{"type":"record","version":2,"saved_at":"2026-09-17T09:51:02+08:00","record":{"name":"Jie An, Ph.D.","current_title":"Research Scientist/Engineer 4 (Senior Scientist)","current_company":"University of Washington, Division of Rheumatology","location":"美国 华盛顿州 西雅图","headline":"Senior Scientist | Immunology, Inflammation & Autoimmune Disease | Translational Drug Discovery | Target Validation & Assay Development | cGAS-STING / Type I IFN","public_url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jie-an-ph-d-76861316","recruiter_url":"https://www.linkedin.com/talent/profile/AEMAAANLUYAB2W95ewCa7sInR98650fI569PXYM","core_skills":["Human Primary Cells","cGAS-STING Signaling","Type I Interferon"],"positions":[{"title":"Research Scientist/Engineer 4 (Senior Scientist)","company":"University of Washington, Division of Rheumatology","date_range":"15 年 1 个月","duration":"7 年 3 个月","location":"美国 华盛顿州 西雅图","description":"• Lead and contribute to mechanism-driven studies of nucleic-acid sensing, cGAS-STING signaling, and type I interferon biology in systemic autoimmune and inflammatory disease. \n \n• Develop and optimize biochemical, cell-based, primary-cell, and in vivo assays for target validation, pathway characterization, mechanism-of-action studies, and candidate-inhibitor evaluation. \n \n• Work extensively with human patient samples and primary immune cells, including PBMCs, neutrophils, monocytes, and pDCs, integrating cellular phenotypes with molecular and biochemical readouts. \n \n• Apply flow cytometry, ELISA/AlphaLISA, qPCR, immunoblotting, IP/Co-IP, microscopy, LC-MS/MS, proteomics, SPR, FRET/HTRF, and in vivo disease models across multidisciplinary research programs. \n \n• Integrate mechanistic wet-lab research with single-cell and spatial transcriptomic studies in inflammatory skin biology and neuroinflammation. \n \n• Mentor research scientists, fellows, postdoctoral researchers, visiting scientists, and student interns in experimental design, troubleshooting, methods, and data interpretation.","skills":["技能: Immunology • Translational Research • Drug Discovery • Inflammation • Assay Development • Target Validation • Human Primary Cells • cGAS-STING Signaling • Type I Interferon • qPCR","Immunology","Translational Research","Drug Discovery","Inflammation","Assay Development","Target Validation","Human Primary Cells","cGAS-STING Signaling","Type I Interferon","qPCR","技能: Translational Research • Drug Discovery • Inflammation • Assay Development • Target Validation • cGAS-STING Signaling • Type I Interferon • qPCR"]},{"title":"Research Assistant Professor of Medicine","company":"University of Washington, Division of Rheumatology","date_range":"15 年 1 个月","duration":"7 年 3 个月","location":"美国 华盛顿州 西雅图","description":"• Lead and contribute to mechanism-driven studies of nucleic-acid sensing, cGAS-STING signaling, and type I interferon biology in systemic autoimmune and inflammatory disease. \n \n• Develop and optimize biochemical, cell-based, primary-cell, and in vivo assays for target validation, pathway characterization, mechanism-of-action studies, and candidate-inhibitor evaluation. \n \n• Work extensively with human patient samples and primary immune cells, including PBMCs, neutrophils, monocytes, and pDCs, integrating cellular phenotypes with molecular and biochemical readouts. \n \n• Apply flow cytometry, ELISA/AlphaLISA, qPCR, immunoblotting, IP/Co-IP, microscopy, LC-MS/MS, proteomics, SPR, FRET/HTRF, and in vivo disease models across multidisciplinary research programs. \n \n• Integrate mechanistic wet-lab research with single-cell and spatial transcriptomic studies in inflammatory skin biology and neuroinflammation. \n \n• Mentor research scientists, fellows, postdoctoral researchers, visiting scientists, and student interns in experimental design, troubleshooting, methods, and data interpretation.","skills":["技能: Immunology • Translational Research • Drug Discovery • Inflammation • Assay Development • Target Validation • Human Primary Cells • cGAS-STING Signaling • Type I Interferon • qPCR","Immunology","Translational Research","Drug Discovery","Inflammation","Assay Development","Target Validation","Human Primary Cells","cGAS-STING Signaling","Type I Interferon","qPCR","技能: Translational Research • Drug Discovery • Inflammation • Assay Development • Target Validation • cGAS-STING Signaling • Type I Interferon • qPCR"]},{"title":"Acting Instructor / Acting Assistant Professor","company":"University of Washington, Division of Rheumatology","date_range":"15 年 1 个月","duration":"7 年 3 个月","location":"美国 华盛顿州 西雅图","description":"• Lead and contribute to mechanism-driven studies of nucleic-acid sensing, cGAS-STING signaling, and type I interferon biology in systemic autoimmune and inflammatory disease. \n \n• Develop and optimize biochemical, cell-based, primary-cell, and in vivo assays for target validation, pathway characterization, mechanism-of-action studies, and candidate-inhibitor evaluation. \n \n• Work extensively with human patient samples and primary immune cells, including PBMCs, neutrophils, monocytes, and pDCs, integrating cellular phenotypes with molecular and biochemical readouts. \n \n• Apply flow cytometry, ELISA/AlphaLISA, qPCR, immunoblotting, IP/Co-IP, microscopy, LC-MS/MS, proteomics, SPR, FRET/HTRF, and in vivo disease models across multidisciplinary research programs. \n \n• Integrate mechanistic wet-lab research with single-cell and spatial transcriptomic studies in inflammatory skin biology and neuroinflammation. \n \n• Mentor research scientists, fellows, postdoctoral researchers, visiting scientists, and student interns in experimental design, troubleshooting, methods, and data interpretation.","skills":["技能: Immunology • Translational Research • Drug Discovery • Inflammation • Assay Development • Target Validation • Human Primary Cells • cGAS-STING Signaling • Type I Interferon • qPCR","Immunology","Translational Research","Drug Discovery","Inflammation","Assay Development