{"type":"record","version":2,"saved_at":"2026-09-17T11:02:05+08:00","record":{"name":"Noelia Che, PhD","current_title":"Research Associate","current_company":"UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health","location":"英国伦敦地区","headline":"Scientific Communicator | Patient‑Centred & Clinician‑Focused Communication | Haematology, Oncology & Genomics","public_url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/noelia-che","recruiter_url":"https://www.linkedin.com/talent/profile/AEMAAArPoFIBEzso4uNg8Wfz2BhMcDGy88SzXfk","core_skills":["Stakeholder & Clinician Engagement","Evidence Synthesis & Critical Appraisal","Clinical Genomics and Genetic Testing Awareness"],"positions":[{"title":"Research Associate","company":"UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health","date_range":"5 年 6 个月","duration":"5 个月","location":"London, England, United Kingdom","description":"Integrated disease biology, biomarker discovery and stakeholder engagement to support evidence‑based therapeutic decisions. Led research, communication and funding activities that strengthened UK haematology networks and advanced preclinical projects towards clinical evaluation. \n \n• Insight generation: Led translational AML research programmes to generate clinically relevant insights, biomarker hypotheses and evidence‑based narratives for haematologists and multidisciplinary teams; provided translational input to preclinical projects moving towards clinical evaluation. \n \n• Scientific communication and relationships: Acted as a scientific point of contact for internal and external experts, communicating complex findings in tailored formats for clinicians, researchers, funders and lay stakeholders; strengthened networks across the UK haematology and oncology community through talks, panels and workshops.  \n \n• Governance and leadership: Developed data‑driven funding cases and secured competitive grants from charities; managed multi‑partner collaborations and applied GCP‑aligned principles and institutional SOPs when handling patient samples and data. \n \nKey Achievements: \n>   Secured £180K+ in competitive funding to support AML research, building persuasive, data‑driven cases for investment from clinical and charitable stakeholders. \n>   Engaged 50+ internal and external experts through invited talks, panel discussions and workshops, strengthening networks across the UK haematology and oncology community. \n>   Mentored 3 MSc students, supporting progression into PhD and industry‑relevant roles. \n>   Provided translational input that helped progress 5 preclinical projects towards clinical evaluation, including identifying potential biomarkers and patient subgroups. \n>   Published 4 peer-reviewed papers, including 2 first-author publications.","skills":["技能: Scientific Engagement • Medical Education • Scientific Communication • Mentorship & Supervision • Digital Learning Platforms • Evidence-based Education","Scientific Engagement","Medical Education","Scientific Communication","Mentorship & Supervision","Digital Learning Platforms","Evidence-based Education","技能: Scientific Engagement • Scientific Communication • Drug Discovery & Therapeutic Prioritisation • Cross-functional Collaborations • Goal-Oriented Project Leadership • Mentorship & Supervision • Multi-omics & Data Integration • Cross-Functional Team Coordination • Translational Research • Strategic Planning","Drug Discovery & Therapeutic Prioritisation","Cross-functional Collaborations","Goal-Oriented Project Leadership","Multi-omics & Data Integration","Cross-Functional Team Coordination","Translational Research","Strategic Planning"]},{"title":"John Goldman Fellow","company":"UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health","date_range":"5 年 6 个月","duration":"5 个月","location":"London, England, United Kingdom","description":"Integrated disease biology, biomarker discovery and stakeholder engagement to support evidence‑based therapeutic decisions. Led research, communication and funding activities that strengthened UK haematology networks and advanced preclinical projects towards clinical evaluation. \n \n• Insight generation: Led translational AML research programmes to generate clinically relevant insights, biomarker hypotheses and evidence‑based narratives for haematologists and multidisciplinary teams; provided translational input to preclinical projects moving towards clinical evaluation. \n \n• Scientific communication and relationships: Acted as a scientific point of contact for internal and external experts, communicating complex findings in tailored formats for clinicians, researchers, funders and lay stakeholders; strengthened networks across the UK haematology and oncology community through talks, panels and workshops.  \n \n• Governance and leadership: Developed data‑driven funding cases and secured competitive grants from charities; managed multi‑partner collaborations and applied GCP‑aligned principles and institutional SOPs when handling patient samples and data. \n \nKey Achievements: \n>   Secured £180K+ in competitive funding to support AML research, building persuasive, data‑driven cases for investment from clinical and charitable stakeholders. \n>   Engaged 50+ internal and external experts through invited talks, panel discussions and workshops, strengthening networks across the UK haematology and oncology community. \n>   Mentored 3 MSc students, supporting progression into PhD and industry‑relevant roles. \n>   Provided translational input that helped progress 5 preclinical projects towards clinical evaluation, including identifying potential biomarkers and patient subgroups. \n>   Published 4 peer-reviewed papers, including 2 first-author publications.","skills":["技能: Scientific Engagement • Medical Education • Scientific Communication • Mentorship & Supervision • Digital Learning Platforms • Evidence-based Education","Scientific Engagement","Medical Education","Scientific Communication","Mentorship & Supervision","Digital Learning Platforms","Evidence-