s biomarker subject matter expert on sponsor program teams, analyzed and interpreted biomarker data to inform asset strategy and translational decisions; collaborated with clinical, pharmacology, and data-management colleagues and with academic partners. \n- Authored biomarker sections of clinical protocols, validation plans, and validation reports under ICH M10, GCP, and GLP; supported Health Authority responses; established SOPs, instrument qualification, and LIMS-based sample tracking (LabVantage, Watson). \n- Provided scientific support to business development through proposals and bid defense; managed timelines, scope, change orders, and sponsor communication. \n- Presented at WRIB 2024 on parallelism assessment for ligand-binding biomarker assay validation. \n \nAlzheimer's, Parkinson's, MS, RA, Lupus, Psoriasis, MM, Acute kidney injury, Obesity and cardiometabolic, Neuromuscular (DMD) and Osteoporosis\n\n• Built the biomarker and PK/PD laboratory function from the ground up (SOPs, instrument qualification, GLP/GCLP/ICH M10 frameworks, LIMS (LabVantage, Watson), and Hamilton/MSD automation). \n• Hired, trained, and managed 7 scientists (3 PhDs). \n• Provided scientific and technical oversight of assay execution and delivery for pharmaceutical sponsors, directing development and fit-for-purpose validation of over 100 clinical biomarker and PK assays (MSD multiplex, ELISA, ligand-binding, qPCR/ddPCR, flow cytometry). \n• Served as sponsor-facing biomarker subject matter expert across autoimmune, neurodegeneration, oncology, cardiometabolic, and inflammatory programs; aligned biomarker plans with each sponsor's mechanism of action and regulatory expectations for Phase I-II. \n• Authored biomarker sections of clinical protocols, Investigator's Brochures, ICFs, validation plans, and analytical study reports (ASRs) aligned with ICH M10, GCP, and GLP. \n• Managed sponsor biomarker projects end-to-end (timelines, budgets exceeding 3 million dollars, contract scope, an